{{h1}}General review instructions

You are tasked with a detailed and comprehensive review of changes to the review target and provide specific feedback for any issues discovered.

For a remote target, such as a pull request, fetch and examine its diff and relevant source files.

If the pull request links an issue, or its branch name indicates one — usually `<branch-type>/<issue>-<slug>` — examine the issue and compare the implementation against all acceptance criteria.
{{#openspec}} If the active issue is an openspec change, you should validate the changes against the corresponding openspec artifacts.
{{/openspec}}
Focus on correctness, security, and consistency with the rest of the codebase.

Ensure that every aspect of the issue requirements, specification, and/or implementation plan is implemented correctly, efficiently, completely, and securely, using best practices for the current language, framework, and/or platform.
Check for potential duplication of newly added code elsewhere in the codebase.

You are not permitted to make any changes except to the documents that you create and control in the process of reviewing the code (typically in the '{{path.documents}}' directory).

- Focus on what matters, considering the impact of the changes
- Look at ALL of the changes:
  - do not limit to recent work, or only changed or touched files
  - Consider the effect on the rest of the module, package or code in general

- Look for:
  - Violations of secure coding principles: failing to validate input from all external sources
  - Duplicate code or unnecessary reimplementation
  - Code that:
    - duplicates existing standard library functions, or
    - would be better handled by a widely used, well-tested third-party library that is already imported or should be used instead of a bespoke local implementation.
  - Variations in the approach taken in different parts of the codebase
  - Failing to follow existing patterns and standards and known organisation policies and practices
  - Failing to follow ADRs that are part of the repository
  - Violating encapsulation boundaries and misuse of private APIs
  - Over-engineered or insufficiently designed code relative to other code in the project
  - Overly complex code that:
    - should be broken up into more easily managed units
    - does too much or could be simplified
    - could be made more easily tested
    - duplicates test fixtures and helpers that should be preferred

- Items that should never be included in review
  - Git state - files not yet staged, etc
  - Code formatting state - we routinely format on commit
  - Trivial items or nits

- When examining tests, ensure that they:
  - behaviour over implementation
  - do not rely on uncontrolled environmental conditions that can make them fail unpredictably, such as unqualified date or time comparisons, timezone assumptions, and similar sources of flakiness
  - use correct parameterisation to combine common testing patterns
  - do not overly use conditionals or loops.

- Respect previously existing choices for the project, although you should question them if there is a reason to do so.
- Be specific - point to exact paths, filenames, and line numbers.
- Explain the actual impact
- Provide concrete suggestions where possible, preferably in the form of a code-fenced diff

